FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:2619 — `collections.deque(list)` builds a deque of any length without checking `MAX_DEQUE_ITEMS`, but every later operation goes through `deque_parts` (src/runtime.rs:1950-1965), which rejects a total over 1,000,000. So a deque of 1,000,001 items can be constructed and then every `pop_*`/`push_*` refuses it with the misleading "a deque is a record made by collections.deque" error; likewise `push_right` at exactly the cap returns an out-of-budget deque. Enforce the same budget in the constructor and after push, or make the cap validation-only so a legitimate large deque is never created unusable. VERIFIED: traced `collections_deque` → `stack_from_values`/`deque_value` with no budget check, then `collections_pop_left` → `deque_parts` → `total > MAX_DEQUE_ITEMS` → `deque_error`.
2. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:1892 — `deque_error` still says "a deque is a record made by collections.deque", but deques are now `Value::Object` (src/runtime.rs:1908) and a hand-written record is the untrusted path. The message names the representation the module now refuses; reword to "a value made by collections.deque" (and update the `"deque is a record"` assertion needles in tests).
3. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:2151 — the `stack_cons` doc comment still claims "Validation of the whole deque still reads every physical node before the structural change", but the resume made module deques skip `validate_stack_tree` in `deque_parts`. The comment describes the old O(n) behaviour and hides the trust boundary; update it to say only the spine's top trees are checked.
4. [MINOR] SPEC.md (uncommitted, collections/deque paragraph) — "a hand-written record with the same fields is still read" omits that the record must also carry `kind: "deque"`; `deque_parts` refuses a record with `front`/`back` but no/other `kind` (src/runtime.rs:1930-1936). Name the marker as REPORT.md does.
5. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:1908 / src/runtime.rs:3935 — deques are now the first source-reachable `Value::Object`, and `json.stringify` renders `Value::Object` as `"null"` (line observed via grep). That means stringifying a record containing a deque silently drops it rather than refusing. Not verified end-to-end; confirm the silent null is intended and document it, or make JSON refuse an opaque deque.

